.TH ATLAS-GREP 1 "2026-11-23" "CyberSci Command Atlas" "Command Atlas"
.SH NAME
atlas\-grep \- Search text (or binary data) for lines that match a pattern, and print them.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
grep  \-i  \-n  \-E  'pattern'  file1 file2
 |    |   |   |     |         '\-\-\- files to search (or a folder with \-r; or stdin if omitted)
 |    |   |   |     '\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\- the pattern (quote it so the shell leaves it alone)
 |    |   |   '\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\- \-E = extended regular expressions (+ ? | () work)
 |    |   '\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\- \-n = print line numbers
 |    '\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\- \-i = ignore upper/lower case
 '\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\- the program
.fi
.SH WHEN TO USE
.IP \(bu 2
Find a flag, password or username inside a log, dump, memory image or whole folder.
.IP \(bu 2
Count or list evidence: failed logins, an IP, a path, a user agent.
.IP \(bu 2
Filter the output of another command (\fBcmd | grep ...\fR).
.SH FLAGS
.TP
\fB\-i\fR
Ignore case.
.br
Use it when: You do not know if it is \fBPassword\fR or \fBPASSWORD\fR.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR
Invert: show lines that do NOT match.
.br
Use it when: Remove the noise to see what is left.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR
Prefix lines with their line number.
.br
Use it when: You need to open the file at that spot later.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR
Count matching lines instead of printing them.
.br
Use it when: How many failures? how many hits?
.TP
\fB\-o\fR
Print only the matching part.
.br
Use it when: Pull out IPs, emails, flags.
.TP
\fB\-E\fR
Extended regex (\fB+ ? | ( )\fR need no backslash).
.br
Use it when: Anything beyond a plain word.
.TP
\fB\-F\fR
Fixed string: treat the pattern as plain text.
.br
Use it when: The pattern has \fB{ } . * [\fR characters you want literally.
.TP
\fB\-w / \-x\fR
Match whole words / whole lines only.
.br
Use it when: \fBroot\fR should not match \fBrootkit\fR.
.TP
\fB\-r / \-R\fR
Recurse into folders (\-R follows symlinks).
.br
Use it when: Search a whole project or extracted archive.
.TP
\fB\-l / \-L\fR
List only file names that do / do not match.
.br
Use it when: Which files contain it?
.TP
\fB\-A N / \-B N / \-C N\fR
Show N lines After / Before / around the match.
.br
Use it when: Read the context of a hit.
.TP
\fB\-a\fR
Treat binary files as text.
.br
Use it when: Search images, binaries, memory dumps.
.TP
\fB\-b\fR
Print the byte offset of each match.
.br
Use it when: Locate data inside a binary for \fBxxd\fR/\fBdd\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-include='*.py' / \-\-exclude\-dir=.git\fR
Limit which files a recursive search reads.
.br
Use it when: Large trees with noise.
.TP
\fB\-P\fR
Perl\-style regex (lookahead, \fB\ed\fR).
.br
Use it when: Complex patterns; not available on every system.
.TP
\fB\-m N\fR
Stop after N matches.
.br
Use it when: Huge files; you only need the first hit.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR
Quiet: no output, just the exit status.
.br
Use it when: Use in \fBif grep \-q ...; then\fR.
.SH RECIPES
.SS "1. Find a word, ignoring case, with line numbers"
Notes mix \fBPassword\fR and \fBPASSWORD\fR. \fB\-i\fR catches both; \fB\-n\fR tells you where.
.PP
.nf
$ grep \-in password notes.txt
.fi
.PP
.nf
2:Password reset for bob: Summer2026!
4:password policy: 12 characters
6:Remember: PASSWORD files are case sensitive?
.fi
.PP
Three lines match even though the capitalisation differs. Without \fB\-i\fR you would miss two of them.
.SS "2. Count failed logins"
\fB\-c\fR prints a number instead of the lines. The spaces around \fB401\fR avoid matching \fB4010\fR or a byte count.
.PP
.nf
$ grep \-c ' 401 ' access.log
.fi
.PP
.nf
3
.fi
.PP
The number is how many lines contain the HTTP status 401 (unauthorised).
.SS "3. Everything except the boring lines"
\fB\-v\fR inverts the match. Drop the normal successes and read what is left.
.PP
.nf
$ grep \-v ' 200 ' access.log
.fi
.PP
.nf
10.0.0.9 \- \- [14/Nov/2026:10:02:01 +0000] "POST /login HTTP/1.1" 401 87
10.0.0.9 \- \- [14/Nov/2026:10:02:03 +0000] "POST /login HTTP/1.1" 401 87
10.0.0.9 \- \- [14/Nov/2026:10:02:05 +0000] "POST /login HTTP/1.1" 401 87
10.0.0.9 \- \- [14/Nov/2026:10:02:09 +0000] "POST /login HTTP/1.1" 302 0
10.0.0.7 \- \- [14/Nov/2026:10:03:40 +0000] "GET /../../etc/passwd HTTP/1.1" 400 0
10.0.0.7 \- \- [14/Nov/2026:10:03:44 +0000] "GET /backup.zip HTTP/1.1" 404 196
.fi
.PP
Failed logins, a redirect, a path traversal attempt and a 404 remain. This is how you triage a log.
.SS "4. Ranked list of source IPs (pipeline)"
\fB\-o\fR prints only the matched IP, \fBsort | uniq \-c\fR counts duplicates, \fBsort \-rn\fR ranks them.
.PP
.nf
$ grep \-oE '^[0\-9]+(\e.[0\-9]+){3}' access.log | sort | uniq \-c | sort \-rn
.fi
.PP
.nf
      5 10.0.0.9
      2 10.0.0.7
      2 10.0.0.5
.fi
.PP
The first line is the noisiest client. The same pattern (match, sort, count, rank) works on usernames, paths and user agents.
.SS "5. Context around a hit"
\fB\-C1\fR shows one line before and after. A 302 after several 401s means the login finally worked.
.PP
.nf
$ grep \-n \-C1 ' 302 ' access.log
.fi
.PP
.nf
5\-10.0.0.9 \- \- [14/Nov/2026:10:02:05 +0000] "POST /login HTTP/1.1" 401 87
6:10.0.0.9 \- \- [14/Nov/2026:10:02:09 +0000] "POST /login HTTP/1.1" 302 0
7\-10.0.0.9 \- \- [14/Nov/2026:10:02:15 +0000] "GET /admin HTTP/1.1" 200 2210
.fi
.PP
Three failures, then the redirect, then the admin page: a brute\-force that succeeded.
.SS "6. Search a whole folder"
\fB\-r\fR descends into folders, \fB\-n\fR adds line numbers. Hidden files (names starting with a dot) are included.
.PP
.nf
$ grep \-rn 'secret' site
.fi
.PP
.nf
site/.env:1:secret=1
.fi
.PP
The \fB.env\fR file shows up with its file name and line number.
.SS "7. Find text inside a binary file"
\fB\-a\fR makes grep read binary as text. This is the fastest check for data appended to an image.
.PP
.nf
$ grep \-a \-o 'flag{[^}]*}' pic.png
.fi
.PP
.nf
flag{demo_not_a_real_flag}
.fi
.PP
A flag\-shaped string was hidden after the end of the picture. (The flag here is a demo value.)
.SS "8. Why \-F matters"
In a regex the dot means 'any single character', so it matches the \fBx\fR too. \fB\-F\fR matches the characters exactly as typed.
.PP
.nf
$ printf 'flag{1x3}\enflag{1.3}\en' > t.txt; echo '\-\-\- regex:'; grep 'flag{1.3}' t.txt; echo '\-\-\- fixed string:'; grep \-F 'flag{1.3}' t.txt
.fi
.PP
.nf
\-\-\- regex:
flag{1x3}
flag{1.3}
\-\-\- fixed string:
flag{1.3}
.fi
.PP
The regex version prints both lines; the \fB\-F\fR version prints only the one with a real dot. Use \fB\-F\fR for anything copied from a log, an IP or a flag.
.SH COMMON MISTAKES
.IP \(bu 2
Quote the pattern: unquoted \fB*\fR and \fB$\fR are expanded by the shell before grep sees them.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBgrep pattern *\fR skips hidden files; use \fB\-r\fR on the folder.
.IP \(bu 2
A dot \fB.\fR in a regex means any character; escape it (\fB\e.\fR) or use \fB\-F\fR for an IP such as \fB10.0.0.5\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
On binary files without \fB\-a\fR, grep says \fBBinary file matches\fR and prints nothing useful.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-P\fR is not in every grep (macOS, BusyBox): \fB\-E\fR is the portable choice.
.SH PRACTICE
.IP 1. 3
Count how many distinct IPs hit \fB/login\fR in \fBaccess.log\fR.
.IP 2. 3
List every file under \fBsite\fR that contains the letter \fBx\fR, with line numbers.
.IP 3. 3
Find the byte offset of the flag text in \fBpic.png\fR, then print the bytes there with \fBxxd \-s\fR.
.SH SEE ALSO
rg (ripgrep: faster, respects .gitignore), awk, sed, find, strings, zgrep (search .gz without extracting).
.SH CAPTURE
Output captured 2026\-11\-23 with: grep (GNU grep) 3.11
